Liberal
	[m1][t]lĭb′ẽr·al[/t][/m]
	[m1][p]a.[/p] [p]Etym:[/p] [com][i]F. libéral, L. liberalis, fr. liber free.[/i][/com][/m]
	[m1]1) Free by birth; hence, befitting a freeman or gentleman; refined; noble. [ex]a liberal ancestry[/ex][/m]
	[m1]2) Bestowing in a large and noble way, as a freeman; generous; bounteous. [ex]a liberal giver[/ex][/m]
	[m2]a) Not strict or rigorous; free; as, a liberal translation of a classic.[/m]
	[m2]b) Not narrow or contracted in mind; catholic.[/m]
	[m1]3) Free to excess; licentious. [p]Obs.[/p][/m]
	[m1]4) Not bound by orthodox tenets; independent in opinion. See <<Liberalism>>.[/m]
	[m1][*][b]Syn.[/b] — Generous; bountiful; munificent; ample; profuse; free.[/*][/m]

Liberalism
	[m1][t]lĭb′ẽr·al·ĭz’m[/t][/m]
	[m1][p]n.[/p][/m]
	[m1]Liberal principles; the principles and methods of the liberals in politics or religion; specifically, the principles of the ~ party. [s]liberalism.wav[/s][/m]

{to} bank
bank
	[m1][p]v. i.[/p][/m]
	[m1]1) To keep a bank; to carry on the business of a banker.[/m]
	[m1]2) To deposit money in a bank; to have an account with a banker. [ref]bank[/ref][/m]
	@ bank up
	[m1]To heap up; as, to bank up the fire.[/m]
	@

bank
	[m1][p]n.[/p] [p]Etym:[/p] [com][i]OE. banke; akin to E. bench.[/i][/com][/m]
	[m1]1) A mound, pile, or ridge of earth, raised above the surrounding level. [ex]We walked along the river bank.[/ex] [url]https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/74[/url][/m]
	[m1]2) A bench, as for rowers in a galley.[/m]
